@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.471 = private unnamed_addr constant [37 x i8] c"unnecessary-literal-within-tuple-call", align 1
@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.472 = private unnamed_addr constant [1040 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `tuple` calls that take unnecessary list or tuple literals as\0Aarguments. In [preview], this also includes unnecessary list comprehensions\0Awithin tuple calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIt's unnecessary to use a list or tuple literal within a `tuple()` call,\0Asince there is a literal syntax for these types.\0A\0AIf a list literal was passed, then it should be rewritten as a `tuple`\0Aliteral. Otherwise, if a tuple literal was passed, then the outer call\0Ato `tuple()` should be removed.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule also checks for list comprehensions within `tuple()`\0Acalls. If a list comprehension is found, it should be rewritten as a\0Agenerator expression.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Atuple([1, 2])\0Atuple((1, 2))\0Atuple([x for x in range(10)])\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A(1, 2)\0A(1, 2)\0Atuple(x for x in range(10))\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may occasionally drop comments\0Awhen rewriting the call. In most cases, though, comments will be preserved.\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
