@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.483 = private unnamed_addr constant [28 x i8] c"call-datetime-without-tzinfo", align 1
@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.484 = private unnamed_addr constant [830 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `datetime` instantiations that do not specify a timezone.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`datetime` objects are \22naive\22 by default, in that they do not include\0Atimezone information. \22Naive\22 objects are easy to understand, but ignore\0Asome aspects of reality, which can lead to subtle bugs. Timezone-aware\0A`datetime` objects are preferred, as they represent a specific moment in\0Atime, unlike \22naive\22 objects.\0A\0ABy providing a non-`None` value for `tzinfo`, a `datetime` can be made\0Atimezone-aware.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime(2000, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime(2000, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)\0A```\0A\0AOr, on Python 3.11 and later:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime(2000, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, tzinfo=datetime.UTC)\0A```\0A", align 1
